<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Education and Learning – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Education and Learning</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Next Trimester, I Plan to Learn...</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Analyze the structure of various texts. Reading: Develop arguments based on one or more texts studied. Phonemic Awareness: Demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by practicing minimal pair sounds.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can analyze the structure of short educational texts (e.g., "Read about a subject and identify the introduction, body, and conclusion."). Can develop arguments based on reading about educational themes (e.g., "What subject would you study more and why?"). Can produce and differentiate between minimal pair sounds, accurately identifying and pronouncing distinct phonemes (e.g., /ɪ/ in "skill" versus /iː/ in "skull," /æ/ in "had" versus /e/ "head").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher begins by showing pictures of various academic subjects and skills (e.g., a student coding, a history book, someone playing an instrument, a math equation). Students discuss in pairs which subjects they find interesting. The teacher asks, "What subject *would you like to learn* more about next trimester?" and encourages short responses using modals. This activates prior vocabulary like 'subject', 'skills', 'trimester'.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models pronunciation and stress patterns for target phrases like "I *would like* to study..." and "I am *learning*..." The teacher also clearly pronounces key vocabulary words from Lesson 1 that will appear in the reading text, such as 'scholarship', 'academic', 'career goals', 'critical'. The teacher might say, "Listen carefully: 'I *would like* to develop new *skills* next *trimester*.'"</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key terms and concepts related to future plans and academic subjects.
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks: "What is a 'trimester'? Is it a full year, half a year, or a third of a year?" (Expected answer: "A third of a year / Part of a year.")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks: "If you have 'career goals', what does that mean? Do you want to work, or just relax?" (Expected answer: "You want to work / have a job in the future.")</li>
            </ul></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Education and Learning"</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short blog post titled "Our Trimester Plans: What We're Learning Next!" featuring three different 8th-grade students discussing their educational plans, using present continuous for ongoing actions, gerunds after prepositions, and modals for desires. The text is structured with a clear introduction, separate paragraphs for each student's plan (body), and a concluding thought. (Register: informal but educational, Format: short blog post).</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading comprehension (analyzing text structure, extracting specific information, understanding main ideas).</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students read the blog post silently for 2 minutes. The teacher asks: "What is the main idea of this text? Is it about food, travel, or school plans?" (Expected answer: "School plans.")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Statements:</b> The teacher provides 3-4 True/False statements related to the students' plans in the text. E.g., "One student is interested in *learning* history." or "A student is *actively* working to *achieve* a *scholarship*." Students mark T or F.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match each student's name from the blog post to their main subject or skill they *would like to develop*. E.g., "Ana -> Computer Science," "Luis -> Math," "Sofia -> English."</li>
            </ul></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading comprehension, text structure analysis, using target grammar (present continuous, gerunds, modals) and vocabulary. Phonemic awareness: minimal pair sounds (/ɪ/ vs /iː/, /æ/ vs /e/).</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Reading for Structure (Controlled Practice):</b> Students re-read the blog post individually. They are given markers or highlighters and instructed to:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Underline the *introduction* of the text in blue.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Draw a box around the *body paragraphs* (where each student's plans are described) in green.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Circle the *conclusion* in red.</li>
                    The teacher monitors and provides assistance, ensuring they correctly identify the structural elements.</li>
                </ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Grammar and Vocabulary Cloze (Semi-controlled):</b> Students complete a cloze activity based on sentences from the blog post. They choose from a word bank including target vocabulary and correct grammatical forms. Example sentences: "She *is learning* computer science and English." "He *is interested in learning* about history." "I *would like to study* math more *actively*."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Minimal Pairs & Reading Aloud (Pair Interaction/Peer Check):</b> The teacher introduces minimal pair sounds relevant to the lesson's vocabulary: /ɪ/ vs /iː/ (e.g., 'skill' vs. 'skull' - *though 'skull' isn't vocabulary, it helps contrast 'skill'*) and /æ/ vs /e/ (e.g., 'had' vs. 'head' - *though 'head' isn't vocab, it helps contrast 'had'*). The teacher models the sounds. Students work in pairs. One student reads a sentence with a minimal pair word chosen (e.g., "She needs to develop her *skill*." or "He *had* a great idea."), and the partner identifies which word was pronounced. Then, they discuss a question related to the text, such as: "Which *subject* mentioned in the text do you think requires the most *skill* to *develop*?" ensuring they differentiate between /ɪ/ in 'skill' and other similar sounds.</li>
            </ul></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Education and Learning"</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small group discussion followed by a brief presentation of an argument.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Description:</b> In groups of 3-4, students will revisit the blog post. Their task is to collectively *develop an argument* to answer one of the following questions, based on the information in the text and their own opinions:
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which student from the blog post has the most *ambitious career goals* for the next trimester, and why?" (They should reference the text, e.g., "Sofia is very *motivated* because she *is actively studying* for a *scholarship*.")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Based on the text, what is the most *critical skill* for students to *develop* in their *education* today, and why?" (They must justify their choice, e.g., "We believe computer science is *critical* because Ana *is learning* it for a future job.")</li>
            </ul>
            Each group will elect a spokesperson to briefly present their argument (2-3 sentences) to the class. The teacher encourages use of target vocabulary (e.g., 'academic', 'curious', 'grow', 'achieve') and grammar (e.g., "We *would like to argue* that...", "They *are developing* their skills."). Pragmatic functions of "discussing goals" and "offering encouragement" are naturally integrated during group work.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group presentations. The teacher notes down effective use of target vocabulary, clarity of arguments, and participation in discussion.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their developed arguments to the class. Following presentations, each student completes an individual 'Exit Ticket'.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Observation):</b>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> (e.g., 'education', 'skills', 'career goals', 'critical', 'develop', 'achieve')</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Argument:</b> Is the argument well-structured and supported by ideas from the text?</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Did students actively participate in their group discussion?</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Phonemic Awareness:</b> Demonstrated ability to differentiate minimal pair sounds when speaking (observed casually).</li>
            </ul></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students write short answers to the following prompts:
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. One important thing I learned about text structure today is...</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. Based on the text, one subject I *would like to study* more is... because... (using target vocabulary like 'knowledge' or 'grow').</li>
            </ul></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did identifying the introduction, body, and conclusion help you understand the text better?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of developing your argument from the text was most challenging, and why?"</li>
            </ul></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students successfully analyzed the text structure and developed arguments. The minimal pair activity was engaging, but some students still struggled with distinguishing /ɪ/ and /iː/. For the next lesson, I need to ensure explicit modeling of the pronunciation and stress patterns continues. The arguments developed were generally well-supported, showing good reading comprehension. I should prepare more varied texts for future reading lessons to sustain engagement.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson focused on reading about students' educational plans and developing arguments. The next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction) will build on this by having students discuss their *own* personal and learning goals with classmates, using the vocabulary and grammar to express and justify their future plans and offer suggestions.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
    </tr>
</table>